Public housing has become the invisible domestic political issue of the new century. Ensuring all Victorians had access to a roof over their heads was a core political value of the postwar period, even though most were able to attain the Australian dream of home ownership. The existence of public housing in Melbourne was made highly visible through the development in the 1960s of inner-suburban high-rise towers. High-rise blocks became synonymous with Housing Commission (as it then was) housing. These days, on the back of a booming real estate market, high rise is more likely to mean high cost, with apartment blocks sprouting around the city centre and fringe.
