The Chicago area has seen more than a few liquidation sales over the years as discount retailers and department stores shut their doors.
So, it seems a bit unusual that a retail chain has opted to return to a market it exited a decade ago--and to the same store in the same shopping center.
That's what will happen later this month when Ames Department Stores Inc., the nation's largest regional discount retailer, opens a new store in Wheaton.
The 81,000-square-foot store in the Main Street Plaza shopping center, at Main Street and Geneva Road, is one of 10 that Ames has opened or plans to open this year in the Chicago area as it continues to push west from the Northeast and Mid-Atlantic states.
Ames, based in Rocky Hill, Conn., is a $4 billion-a-year full-line discount retailer with about 470 stores and 35,000 employees in 19 states and the District of Columbia.
This is the company's second venture into the Chicago market.
The first ended unhappily in 1990 when losses forced Ames to rid itself of the troubled Zayre chain that it had acquired two years earlier. Its new site in Wheaton is a former Zayre store that was briefly an Ames store, then a Handy Andy and an Aisle 3.
"I wasn't here when they did all that in the first go-around," said Joseph R. Ettore, Ames' chairman and chief executive officer who signed on six years ago. "We're a real different company than we were in 1989 or 1990."
Wheaton offered no incentives to attract Ames, which is entering the suburban market just as Kmart Inc., one of its competitors, is preparing to close several stores.
Donald Rose, Wheaton city manager, said the space that Ames will reoccupy has been difficult to fill.
"It's not a big, big box, but it's bigger than most of the retail spaces," Rose said. "It's a nice little catch for the shopping center."
Despite a tough retail climate for much of the past six months, Ames has managed to compete against the larger discount chains like Kmart, Wal-Mart Stores Inc., and Target Corp. by focusing on customers 55 and older and those with annual incomes between $25,000 and $35,000, said Steven A. Richter, an analyst with Tucker Anthony, a Boston-based investments firm.
"Ames has changed dramatically since 10 years ago," Richter said. "It has been growing pretty dramatically over the last year and a half. Entering the Chicago market is a natural extension of the Ames business model."
The typical Ames store, at 60,000- to 65,000-square-feet, is smaller than its big-box competitors.
Ettore said Ames targets customers who have neither the time nor the inclination to "navigate" larger stores.
"The trick to being successful today is to do things just different enough from Wal-Mart or Kmart," Ettore said. "We have a niche strategy and it works for us."
Stores in Wheaton and three other suburbs--at Bridgeview Court in Bridgeview, the Hillcrest Shopping Center in Crest Hill and at the Canterbury Shopping Center in Markham--are scheduled to open Sept. 21. The Wheaton store is expected to employ 100 to 140 people.
