Sean Barrette describes his perfect day as waking up and kissing his wife, throwing on a pair of jeans, fetching his guitar and writing for a living. Not long ago, the Copper Cliff-based singer-songwriter did a good deed in the hopes that a few others will have a few perfect days.
The self-admitted "bleeding heart" recently spent a cold day busking along Durham Street and collecting money, which he donated to the Samaritan Centre.
"In our society, with all the riches that we have and everything, I don��t really understand how it is that some have so much and some so little," he says. "It��s very important to me to try and help people. Everybody��s got a story and most people are not there of their own volition. Many of them had lives before this came to pass. I just think it��s not such a long drop to the bottom."
Barrette is not certain how much he collected, but at the end of the day the coins in his guitar case filled a large yogurt container.
"I didn��t count it before I took it over, I just kind of gave it to (Kevin Serviss) and said, ��well, here you go��," he explains. "I��m hoping that if people see the video, it might make them interested in finding out what the Samaritan Centre does."
Sisters and Brothers, the song Barrette performs in the video, is simply the latest in a long-running theme that has defined his musical career.
With lyrics such as "Some seek solutions/Some just can��t be bothered/You see the other/But I see sisters and brothers," the song is a call to arms to Sudburians — get involved and volunteer, recycle and donate old clothing and household items, help out someone who could benefit from a few previously-loved garments.
"My wife and I believe in social justice, and helping people who are down-and-out is important to both of us," he says. "A lot of my songs fall into that subject matter. I wrote a song on the first album called Downtown Blues, which was about that. I sort of revisited the theme with Sisters and Brothers."
A nuisance to some, Barrette says busking is his way of helping others in need.
"We��re all in this together; we��ve lost this sense of oneness," he muses. "We all have to help each other out — we��re not a bunch of separate people walking around in our own little bubbles."
It has become a family practice. Barrette and his wife have instilled in their two children an awareness of community and mutual assistance.
"We��re very honest with our kids," he says. "They ask questions and we have a lot of discussions about (social justice work). They��re aware of our charitable work, donations, that kind of thing. They love taking canned goods to their school concerts, and they understand why they��re doing it. It��s just a function of being really honest with your kids, about them feeling grateful for what they have, because a lot of people don��t have what they have."
You can watch Barrette perform his philanthropic tune online at http://tinyurl.com/m8jylzd. His two kids even make cameo appearances near the end of the video.
Barrette has two albums to his name so far: Live Through This, which was released in 2011, and 2013��s Signs, which includes Sisters and Brothers. To learn more about Barrette��s music, go to www.seanbarrette.ca.
