Pembroke Pines Mayor Alex Fekete said he wants to negotiate with Broward's school superintendant rather than let the city's offers to relieve overcrowding go down in defeat.
But Fekete, whose city has been offering to build and pay for schools for the Broward School District since last May, may have a hard time. Superintendent Frank Petruzielo on Tuesday issued terms he called non-negotiable.
Petruzielo's proposal included building a middle school in Miramar with $20 million in impact fees from developers and one elementary school in Pembroke Pines.
But the school in Pembroke Pines would only be built if the city loans the School Board $13 million and agrees not to demand repayment if voters reject a proposed $800 million bond issue.
Fekete called that offer unacceptable.
"The most troubling aspect is that he's saying it's OK as long as we put our taxpayers' money at risk, without any assurance of repayment," Fekete said. "We have no statutory authority to raise taxes for schools. Our residents already pay taxes to the School Board."
Pembroke Pines property owners, he said, are paying $32.5 million to the School Board this year. They are paying $11.4 million to the city.
But Fekete was troubled by several questions, including:
-- How Petruzielo could issue an ultimatum to the cities. "How does he, as superintendant, propose a policy that's non-negotiable?"
-- Why it would cost the School Board $13 million to build a school the city could build for $11.7 million.?
-- Why does Petruzielo object to the city building the schools and leasing them to the School Board? This would require no borrowing by the School Board and a miniumum savings of $15 million over five years. "Where is a realistic comparison with our plan? Why has there been no contact with us? Where is the sincerity?"
Despite this, Fekete said he wants to discuss the issues rather than close the door.
"I'd like to believe there's always room for negotiation when two parties sit at the same table to talk," Fekete said. "If we could make a sincere attempt to work it out, I'm sure we could find some happy medium."
But he said Petruzielo wants to put the cities on the spot.
