BLACKSBURG — Virginia Tech starting right guard Augie Conte isn't one to compare and contrast degrees of gameday bliss against Virginia. He's had his share of it in the rivalry, but those reminiscences are for the rocking chair and the grandkids.
After 12 consecutive seasons of smiles, high-fives and helmets lifted high in celebration when the clock has flipped to zero for the final time in games against U.Va., Tech (8-3, 5-2 ACC) seems to have a permanent read on how to best torment its Commonwealth neighbors on an annual basis. Results may speak for themselves, but Conte insists he and his teammates don't rely on confidence born of past success.
"We take this game as seriously as we do because we want to beat U.Va.," said Conte regarding not worrying about trying to avoid being the Tech team that finally sees the winning streak against U.Va. (2-9, 1-6) come to an end.
"The past 12 years that we've beaten them, all the stats, all the touchdowns, all the interceptions aren't going to help us win this year's game," Conte continued. "I really don't have pressure of being that team. Obviously, you don't want to be that team. I feel like the pressure's more on beating U.Va. because they're U.Va."
Trying to beat U.Va. for a 13th straight season is important for Tech, but in the grand scheme, there's the even bigger matter of finishing business in the ACC's Coastal Division.
A win Friday by North Carolina State against North Carolina will give Tech its first division title since 2011 and a date to play Dec. 3 in the ACC championship game against Clemson. If UNC wins Friday, Tech must beat U.Va. on Saturday to get to the conference championship game.
"I'm excited about our team, and really regardless of what happens on Saturday," Tech coach Justin Fuente said. "I also understand what this rivalry means, maybe to a lot of people more than the Coastal Division championship."
Perhaps part of Conte's singular focus on Saturday's game stems from the fact that despite Tech's lopsided recent series advantage, none of its wins against U.Va. during his time in Blacksburg have come easy.
Tech has either been tied with or trailed U.Va. with less than two minutes left in three of the last four meetings. Of course, Tech during those four seasons wasn't nearly as strong as it was in the previous eight seasons, managing to stay above.500 from 2012-15 (29-23 overall) after winning 10-plus games every season from 2004-2011.
Last season, Tech scored a game-tying touchdown with 8:40 left on a 32-yard pass from quarterback Michael Brewer to wide receiver Isaiah Ford, before Joey Slye booted a game-winning 41-yard field goal with 1:38 remaining to turn a fourth-quarter deficit into a 23-20 win for Tech.
Two years ago, Brewer tossed a 9-yard touchdown pass to tight end Bucky Hodges with 1:48 left to propel Tech to a 24-20 win.
In 2013, neither team scored in the second half, and Tech held on for a 16-6 victory for its 10th straight win in the series, inspiring Hokies fans to fill Scott Stadium with chants of "Ten more years, 10 more years, 10 more years" after the game.
Virginia Tech's 2017 season in photos. Purchase pictures by Daily Press photographers.
Cody Journell pulled out a 17-14 win for Tech in the '12 season when he nailed a 29-yard field goal as time expired.
After so many years of success against their primary rival, and though U.Va. is struggling through a dismal fall this season, Tech's players insist the past dozen or so years haven't put a damper on enthusiasm in the locker room and on the practice field for the coming weekend.
"Of course, with the rivalry week, there's some extra energy in there," Tech defensive tackle Nigel Williams said. "We still prepare the same way in practice....It's an important game because it's the next one."
Virginia Tech's focus could be a byproduct of both last weekend's 34-31 win at Notre Dame after the Hokies fell behind 17-0, plus their failure at home two weekends ago against Georgia Tech.
A win against Georgia Tech would've wrapped up the division and taken all the pressure off the Hokies down the stretch, but the Yellow Jackets, who entered Lane Stadium as double-digit underdogs like the Cavaliers will be this weekend, instead came away with a stunning 30-20 win.
