BOSTON — Democrat Maura Healey says if elected as the next attorney general of Massachusetts, her office would take a proactive approach toward tackling issues plaguing the justice system, from controversial mandatory minimum sentences for non-violent drug offenders and the lack of mental health treatment options for inmates.
In a plan unveiled on Thursday, Healey said the next attorney general must take action to address the way people are processed through the criminal justice system to better serve them and society as a whole.
"We cannot incarcerate and imprison our way to a healthier, more productive state. We know that other approaches work and we must use that knowledge to redirect our resources," Healey said in a statement. "We need to reform our criminal justice system to serve communities and neighborhoods trapped in generations of poverty and cycles of incarceration. We have thousands of men and women in Massachusetts who are put in prison when what they really need is treatment, a diversion program, or a hospital bed."
Related Story: Massachusetts residents overwhelmingly support reforming criminal justice system to focus on rehabilitation over punishment
Among her priorities, Healey, who served in current Attorney General Martha Coakely's office as the chief of the Civil Rights Division, said she would work to reform the bail system, so poor people aren't jailed awaiting trial simply because of their inability to pay. Healey also said that addressing the ongoing opiate crisis would include not just addressing sentencing guidelines, but also expanding access to drug courts which divert those accused of drug-related crimes to treatment rather than jail.
"I'm proud to endorse Maura Healey for Attorney General and stand behind her comprehensive plan for needed criminal justice reform in Massachusetts,�� said Franklin County Sheriff Christopher Donelan in a statement. "Maura understands that we cannot continue cycles of incarceration. In Franklin County and across Massachusetts, we need to focus on substance abuse treatment, mental health services and successful reintegration."
Healey said another focus of her office, if elected, would involve allocating additional resources to programs which help inmates and the recently released reintegrate into society, such as the programs administered under retiring Hampden County Sheriff Michael Ashe.
